  "response": "**Setup**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D+) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(D–) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(T+|D+) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(T–|D–) = 0.95, so false positive rate P(T+|D–) = 0.05\n\n**Bayes' theorem**\n\nP(T+) = P(T+|D+)·P(D+) + P(T+|D–)·P(D–)\nP(T+) = (0.98)(0.002) + (0.05)(0.998)\nP(T+) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\nP(D+|T+) = [P(T+|D+)·P(D+)] / P(T+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794\n\n**Result**\n\nSo even with a positive test, the probability of actually having the condition is only about 3.78% — because the condition is rare, most positives are false positives from the 99.8% who are healthy.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
